  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n- Dry through Tuesday\n\nHigh pressure remains in place through Tuesday with us sandwiched\nbetween the nor'easter to the east and trough to the west. \nSimilar to this morning, fog will be possible early Monday morning\ntoward the Lansing and Jackson areas. Fog could be as dense as \nthis morning with visibilities around a quarter of a mile. If fog \ndevelops be sure to use low-beam headlights and give yourself \nplenty of distance and time for the morning commute.\n\nHighs will be in the 70s with lows in the 40s. A few spots along and \nnorth of US-10 may drop into the upper 30s.\n\n- Rain Chances Return Wednesday into Thursday\n\nThe ridge overhead will slowly weaken. The timing of this \noccurring is trending slower as seen in the onset of precipitation\nchances in the ensembles. As we move into more southerly flow \nsome showers may develop late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning\nwith the best chances (greater than 40 percent) north of a \nHolland to Grand Rapids to Alma line. With the recent trends it is\npossible most could stay dry until Wednesday night into Thursday \nas the front moves in with precipitation chances increasing into \nthe 60 to 70 percent range). \n\nHighs in the 70s with lows in the 50s and 60s are expected.\n\n- Fair Weather Again for Friday and Saturday\n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the cold front Thursday bringing \ndry and cooler conditions for Friday and Saturday. Highs are only \nexpected to be in the 60s with the coldest temperatures Saturday \nmorning in the mid 30s to 40s.",
